 us that the  relation between cause and effect and  our idea  of necessity and causation arises  entirely from the uniformity  observable in the operations of nature  where similar objects are constantly  conjoined together  and the mind is determined by custom to  infer the one from the appearance  of the other so if you've read earlier  parts of the inquiry this is  you know sort of going over old ground  we have a  constant conjunction and then we have a  customary connection  in the mind sort of replicating that  leading us to go from  what we presume to be the cause to the  effect and we call that  cause and effect or causality or in this  case necessity the idea  is that the cause necessitates the  effect and so he tells us these two  circumstances form the whole  of that necessity which we ascribe to  matter to material objects beyond the  constant conjunction of similar objects  and the consequent  inference from one to the other we have  no notion he says  of any necessity of connection  so we can go from this  about matter to thinking about how  our minds and bodies interact  how i decide to lift my hand at this  point in time  and we can also think about the interior  operations of the mind  as well along similar lines and so he  says  all men kind of have ever allowed  without any doubt or hesitation that  these two circumstances  take place in the voluntary actions of  men and in the operations of mind  if it if that's the case it must follow  that all  human beings have always agreed in the  doctrine of necessity  and why have they disagreed with each  other  merely for not understanding each other  now  that's you know that's one way to look  at it and so we've got these two  key things that are coming up throughout  this section  constant conjunction of similar objects  we observe that  right and then inference by custom from  one  object to another easy enough to observe  in the outside world  we can also observe this in the  interactions between  