ssity  and their whole practice and reasoning  have yet discovered such a reluctance to  acknowledge it in words  and have rather shown a propensity to  profess the contrary opinion  why why is it that human beings not just  now  but throughout all history want to say  no i'm actually free  and you're free too well this has to do  as he says with a kind of ignorance  he says that uh if we examine the  operations of the body and the  production of effects from their causes  we find that all our faculties can never  carry us any further in our knowledge of  this relation  than barely to observe that objects are  constantly conjoined together  the mind is carried by this customary  transition from the appearance of one to  the other  to the belief of the other and then he  says  when we turn our reflections towards our  the operations of our own minds  and we ask ourselves okay what's going  on here  he says that we have motives as a matter  of fact everybody has motives  multiple motives in play at any given  time  but we don't feel and he uses that word  feel  it's very important because belief is a  matter of feeling right  we don't feel the connection between the  motive which is the cause  and the action which is the effect  so we think ourselves more free than we  are  you know great example of this is when  you talk to somebody  who's been taken in by a con man  and you ask them did you make these  decisions of your own free will  and after a bit of being ashamed and  saying no he forced me and all that  they'll probably say  yeah i'm such a dummy i did this thing i  did this thing i shouldn't have done  that  oh i feel so bad about it hume would say  well the con man was able to get the  responses that they wanted from you  because they understood how human nature  worked  and how to make suckers apart from their  money and they push the right  buttons they involve the right motives  and  actions so really you're not  uh that responsible for it you just  don't 